/-!
# The normalization of the two-branch multicross ring

Let `k` be a field and let `A = {(f, g) ∈ k[[t]] × k[[t]] : f(0) = g(0)}` be the two-branch
multicross ring, which is `k[[x, y]]/(xy)` by
`Stacks.Curves.quotientAlgEquivMulticross`. This file computes the normalization of `A`: the
integral closure of `A` in its total ring of fractions is the whole of `k[[t]] × k[[t]]`
(`Stacks.Curves.integralClosureMulticrossAlgEquiv`). This is the computation
`A' = κ[[x, y]]/(x) × κ[[x, y]]/(y)` in Case I of the proof of Stacks Tag 0C4A.

The computation has three independent inputs.

* The total ring of fractions of `A` is the product of two copies of the fraction field of
  `k[[t]]` (`Stacks.Curves.isFractionRing_multicross`). A nonzerodivisor of `A` has both
  branches nonzero, because the tuple that is `t` on one branch and `0` on the other lies in
  `A`; and a pair of fractions has a common denominator of the shape `t · d`, whose two
  branches agree and whose numerators are divisible by `t`, so that both numerator and
  denominator lie in `A`.
* `k[[t]] × k[[t]]` is a finite `A`-module, generated by `1` and the idempotent `(1, 0)`
  (`Stacks.Curves.span_one_firstBranchIdem_eq_top`): subtracting the difference of the two
  constant coefficients on the first branch lands in `A`.
* `k[[t]] × k[[t]]` is integrally closed in the product of the fraction fields
  (`Stacks.Curves.isIntegrallyClosedIn_pi_powerSeries`), because integrality is a branchwise
  condition and `k[[t]]` is a unique factorization domain.

Case I of Stacks Tag 0C4A assumes only that the nondegenerate binary quadratic form `q` splits
over the residue field, not that the residue field is algebraically closed. The
classification `Stacks.Curves.nonempty_algEquiv_multicross_iff` requires `[IsAlgClosed k]`, so
the file also proves the split version over an arbitrary field
(`Stacks.Curves.nonempty_algEquiv_multicross_of_isotropic`). Algebraic closedness is used in the
proof of that classification at exactly one point — to produce a root of the dehomogenized quadratic — so the
hypothesis replacing it is that the form has a nontrivial zero `(u, v) ≠ (0, 0)`, which is the
coordinate-free form of "`q` splits over `k`"; in characteristic `2` nondegeneracy reads `b ≠ 0`
and the same two cases work, exactly as for the algebraically closed version.

Two numerical consequences are read off. The quotient of `k[[t]] × k[[t]]` by the multicross
ring is a simple module — a pair outside the multicross ring differs from one inside it by a
nonzero constant multiple of the idempotent, and a nonzero constant is invertible in the
multicross ring — so the delta invariant is `1`
(`Stacks.Curves.deltaInvariant_multicross`). And `k[[t]] × k[[t]]` has exactly two maximal
ideals, one per branch (`Stacks.Curves.encard_maximalSpectrum_pi_powerSeries`): the two branch
idempotents multiply to zero, so a prime ideal contains one of them, and then the ideal is
forced to be the maximal ideal of the other branch. Both are the verification "by hand" of
Case I in the proof of Stacks Tag 0C4A.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.Curves.firstBranchIdem` — the idempotent of `k[[t]] × k[[t]]` supported on the first
  branch.
* `Stacks.Curves.integralClosureMulticrossAlgEquiv` — the normalization of the two-branch
  multicross ring is `k[[t]] × k[[t]]`.
* `Stacks.Curves.branchMaximalIdeal` — the maximal ideal of `k[[t]] × k[[t]]` of one branch.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Curves.isFractionRing_multicross` — the total ring of fractions of the multicross
  ring.
* `Stacks.Curves.span_one_firstBranchIdem_eq_top` — module finiteness of the two branches.
* `Stacks.Curves.isIntegrallyClosedIn_pi_powerSeries` — the two branches are integrally closed.
* `Stacks.Curves.isIntegralClosure_pi_powerSeries` — the two branches are the integral closure.
* `Stacks.Curves.deltaInvariant_multicross` — the delta invariant of the multicross ring is one.
* `Stacks.Curves.eq_branchMaximalIdeal` — a maximal ideal of `k[[t]] × k[[t]]` is a branch
  maximal ideal.
* `Stacks.Curves.encard_maximalSpectrum_pi_powerSeries` — `k[[t]] × k[[t]]` has exactly two
  maximal ideals.
* `Stacks.Curves.exists_linearFactorization_of_isotropic` — a nondegenerate binary quadratic
  form with a nontrivial zero is a product of two independent linear forms, over any field.
* `Stacks.Curves.nonempty_algEquiv_multicross_of_isotropic` — a split nondegenerate quadratic
  hypersurface is the two-branch multicross ring, over any field.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0C4A](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0C4A)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0C4D](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0C4D)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

universe u

namespace Stacks.Curves

open PowerSeries

variable {k : Type u} [Field k]

/-! ### Nonzerodivisors -/

/-- Both branches of a nonzerodivisor of the two-branch multicross ring are nonzero: the tuple
supported on one branch and equal to `t` there is a nonzero element of the multicross ring
killed by any element vanishing on that branch. -/
theorem ne_zero_apply_of_mem_nonZeroDivisors {f : ↥(multicross k 2)}
    (hf : f ∈ nonZeroDivisors ↥(multicross k 2)) (i : Fin 2) :
    (f : Fin 2 → PowerSeries k) i ≠ 0 := by
  intro h0
  have hg : branchSubst (k := k) i ∈ multicross k 2 := by
    rw [mem_multicross]
    intro a b
    have hz : ∀ c : Fin 2, PowerSeries.constantCoeff (branchSubst (k := k) i c) = 0 := by
      intro c
      rcases eq_or_ne c i with rfl | h
      · rw [branchSubst_self]
        simp
      · rw [branchSubst_of_ne h]
        simp
    rw [hz a, hz b]
  have hmul : (⟨branchSubst (k := k) i, hg⟩ : ↥(multicross k 2)) * f = 0 := by
    refine Subtype.ext (funext fun j ↦ ?_)
    rcases eq_or_ne j i with rfl | h
    · simp only [Subring.coe_mul, Pi.mul_apply, h0, mul_zero]
      rfl
    · simp only [Subring.coe_mul, Pi.mul_apply, branchSubst_of_ne h, zero_mul]
      rfl
  have := (mem_nonZeroDivisors_iff.1 hf).2 _ hmul
  have hx : branchSubst (k := k) i i = 0 := congrFun (congrArg Subtype.val this) i
  rw [branchSubst_self] at hx
  exact PowerSeries.X_ne_zero hx

/-- A tuple all of whose entries are nonzero and which lies in the multicross ring is a
nonzerodivisor there. -/
theorem mem_nonZeroDivisors_of_forall_ne_zero {f : ↥(multicross k 2)}
    (hf : ∀ i, (f : Fin 2 → PowerSeries k) i ≠ 0) :
    f ∈ nonZeroDivisors ↥(multicross k 2) := by
  have key : ∀ g : ↥(multicross k 2), g * f = 0 → g = 0 := by
    refine fun g hg ↦ Subtype.ext (funext fun i ↦ ?_)
    have h : (g : Fin 2 → PowerSeries k) i * (f : Fin 2 → PowerSeries k) i = 0 :=
      congrFun (congrArg Subtype.val hg) i
    exact (mul_eq_zero.1 h).resolve_right (hf i)
  exact mem_nonZeroDivisors_iff.2 ⟨fun g hg ↦ key g (by rw [mul_comm]; exact hg), key⟩

/-- A constant tuple lies in the two-branch multicross ring. -/
private theorem mem_multicross_const (p : PowerSeries k) :
    (fun _ : Fin 2 ↦ p) ∈ multicross k 2 :=
  (mem_multicross _).2 fun _ _ ↦ rfl

/-- The action of the multicross ring on `k[[t]] × k[[t]]` is branchwise multiplication. -/
private theorem multicross_smul_apply (a : ↥(multicross k 2)) (b : Fin 2 → PowerSeries k)
    (j : Fin 2) : (a • b) j = (a : Fin 2 → PowerSeries k) j * b j :=
  rfl

/-! ### The total ring of fractions -/

/-- The structure map of the multicross ring into the product of the fraction fields is
branchwise the structure map of a single branch. -/
theorem algebraMap_multicross_apply (a : ↥(multicross k 2)) (i : Fin 2) :
    algebraMap ↥(multicross k 2) (Fin 2 → FractionRing (PowerSeries k)) a i =
      algebraMap (PowerSeries k) (FractionRing (PowerSeries k))
        ((a : Fin 2 → PowerSeries k) i) :=
  rfl

/-- The structure map of the multicross ring into the product of the fraction fields is
injective. -/
theorem algebraMap_multicross_injective :
    Function.Injective
      (algebraMap ↥(multicross k 2) (Fin 2 → FractionRing (PowerSeries k))) := by
  intro x y h
  refine Subtype.ext (funext fun i ↦ ?_)
  have hi := congrFun h i
  rw [algebraMap_multicross_apply, algebraMap_multicross_apply] at hi
  exact FaithfulSMul.algebraMap_injective (PowerSeries k) (FractionRing (PowerSeries k)) hi

/-- **The total ring of fractions of the two-branch multicross ring is the product of two
copies of the field of fractions of `k[[t]]`.** A nonzerodivisor of the multicross ring has both
branches nonzero, hence becomes invertible; conversely a pair of fractions is brought to a
common denominator of the form `t · d`, whose two branches agree and whose numerators are
divisible by `t`, so that both lie in the multicross ring. -/
theorem isFractionRing_multicross :
    IsFractionRing ↥(multicross k 2) (Fin 2 → FractionRing (PowerSeries k)) := by
  classical
  refine (isLocalization_iff (nonZeroDivisors ↥(multicross k 2))
    (Fin 2 → FractionRing (PowerSeries k))).2
    ⟨fun y ↦ ?_, fun z ↦ ?_, fun {x y} h ↦ ⟨1, by rw [algebraMap_multicross_injective h]⟩⟩
  · refine Pi.isUnit_iff.2 fun i ↦ ?_
    rw [algebraMap_multicross_apply]
    refine isUnit_iff_ne_zero.2 fun h0 ↦ ne_zero_apply_of_mem_nonZeroDivisors y.2 i ?_
    exact FaithfulSMul.algebraMap_injective (PowerSeries k) (FractionRing (PowerSeries k))
      (by rw [h0, map_zero])
  · choose w hw using fun i ↦ IsLocalization.surj (nonZeroDivisors (PowerSeries k)) (z i)
    set p : Fin 2 → PowerSeries k := fun i ↦ (w i).1 with hp
    set dd : Fin 2 → PowerSeries k := fun i ↦ ((w i).2 : PowerSeries k) with hdd
    set D : PowerSeries k := dd 0 * dd 1 with hD
    set E : Fin 2 → PowerSeries k := fun i ↦ if i = 0 then dd 1 else dd 0 with hE
    have hkey : ∀ i, dd i * E i = D := by
      intro i
      fin_cases i
      · simp [hE, hD]
      · simp [hE, hD, mul_comm]
    have hddne : ∀ i, dd i ≠ 0 := fun i ↦ nonZeroDivisors.coe_ne_zero (w i).2
    have hn : (fun i ↦ PowerSeries.X * E i * p i) ∈ multicross k 2 := by
      rw [mem_multicross]
      intro a b
      simp [map_mul, PowerSeries.constantCoeff_X]
    refine ⟨⟨⟨_, hn⟩, ⟨⟨_, mem_multicross_const (PowerSeries.X * D)⟩,
      mem_nonZeroDivisors_of_forall_ne_zero fun i ↦ ?_⟩⟩, ?_⟩
    · exact mul_ne_zero PowerSeries.X_ne_zero (mul_ne_zero (hddne 0) (hddne 1))
    · refine funext fun i ↦ ?_
      have hz := hw i
      simp only [Pi.mul_apply, algebraMap_multicross_apply]
      rw [← hkey i, map_mul, map_mul, map_mul, map_mul]
      linear_combination (algebraMap (PowerSeries k) (FractionRing (PowerSeries k))
        PowerSeries.X * algebraMap (PowerSeries k) (FractionRing (PowerSeries k)) (E i)) * hz

/-! ### Finiteness of the two branches over the multicross ring -/

/-- The idempotent of `k[[t]] × k[[t]]` supported on the first branch. -/
def firstBranchIdem (k : Type u) [Field k] : Fin 2 → PowerSeries k :=
  fun i ↦ if i = 0 then 1 else 0

/-- Every pair is a multicross multiple of `1` plus the constant difference of its two constant
coefficients times the idempotent of the first branch. -/
private theorem exists_smul_one_add_smul_firstBranchIdem (f : Fin 2 → PowerSeries k) :
    ∃ a : ↥(multicross k 2), a • (1 : Fin 2 → PowerSeries k) +
      (⟨_, mem_multicross_const (PowerSeries.C (PowerSeries.constantCoeff (f 0) -
        PowerSeries.constantCoeff (f 1)))⟩ : ↥(multicross k 2)) • firstBranchIdem k = f := by
  have hm : (fun i ↦ f i - PowerSeries.C (PowerSeries.constantCoeff (f i)) +
      PowerSeries.C (PowerSeries.constantCoeff (f 1))) ∈ multicross k 2 := by
    rw [mem_multicross]
    intro a b
    simp
  refine ⟨⟨_, hm⟩, funext fun i ↦ ?_⟩
  rw [Pi.add_apply, multicross_smul_apply, multicross_smul_apply]
  fin_cases i <;> simp [firstBranchIdem, map_sub]

/-- **`k[[t]] × k[[t]]` is generated as a module over the two-branch multicross ring by `1` and
the idempotent of the first branch.** Subtracting from a pair the constant series with value the
difference of its two constant coefficients, placed on the first branch, lands in the multicross
ring. -/
theorem span_one_firstBranchIdem_eq_top :
    Submodule.span ↥(multicross k 2)
        ({1, firstBranchIdem k} : Set (Fin 2 → PowerSeries k)) = ⊤ := by
  refine top_le_iff.1 fun f _ ↦ ?_
  obtain ⟨a, ha⟩ := exists_smul_one_add_smul_firstBranchIdem f
  exact Submodule.mem_span_pair.2 ⟨a, _, ha⟩

/-- `k[[t]] × k[[t]]` is a finite module over the two-branch multicross ring. -/
instance moduleFinite_multicross :
    Module.Finite ↥(multicross k 2) (Fin 2 → PowerSeries k) := by
  classical
  exact ⟨⟨{1, firstBranchIdem k}, by
    simpa using span_one_firstBranchIdem_eq_top (k := k)⟩⟩

/-- `k[[t]] × k[[t]]` is integral over the two-branch multicross ring. -/
instance isIntegral_multicross :
    Algebra.IsIntegral ↥(multicross k 2) (Fin 2 → PowerSeries k) :=
  Algebra.IsIntegral.of_finite _ _

/-! ### The two branches form the integral closure -/

/-- The structure map of `k[[t]] × k[[t]]` into the product of the fraction fields is
injective. -/
theorem algebraMap_pi_powerSeries_injective :
    Function.Injective (algebraMap (Fin 2 → PowerSeries k)
      (Fin 2 → FractionRing (PowerSeries k))) := fun _ _ h ↦
  funext fun i ↦ FaithfulSMul.algebraMap_injective (PowerSeries k)
    (FractionRing (PowerSeries k)) (congrFun h i)

/-- **`k[[t]] × k[[t]]` is integrally closed in the product of the fraction fields.** An integral
element is integral branchwise, and `k[[t]]` is a unique factorization domain, hence integrally
closed. -/
theorem isIntegrallyClosedIn_pi_powerSeries :
    IsIntegrallyClosedIn (Fin 2 → PowerSeries k)
      (Fin 2 → FractionRing (PowerSeries k)) := by
  classical
  refine ⟨algebraMap_pi_powerSeries_injective, fun {x} ↦ ⟨fun hx ↦ ?_, ?_⟩⟩
  · have hcomp : ∀ i, ∃ y : PowerSeries k,
        algebraMap (PowerSeries k) (FractionRing (PowerSeries k)) y = x i := by
      intro i
      refine IsIntegrallyClosed.isIntegral_iff.1 ?_
      refine IsIntegral.map_of_comp_eq
        (Pi.evalRingHom (fun _ : Fin 2 ↦ PowerSeries k) i)
        (Pi.evalRingHom (fun _ : Fin 2 ↦ FractionRing (PowerSeries k)) i) (RingHom.ext fun _ ↦ rfl)
        hx
    choose y hy using hcomp
    exact ⟨y, funext hy⟩
  · rintro ⟨y, rfl⟩
    exact isIntegral_algebraMap

/-- **The integral closure of the two-branch multicross ring in the product of the fraction
fields is `k[[t]] × k[[t]]`.** -/
theorem isIntegralClosure_pi_powerSeries :
    IsIntegralClosure (Fin 2 → PowerSeries k) ↥(multicross k 2)
      (Fin 2 → FractionRing (PowerSeries k)) :=
  let := isIntegrallyClosedIn_pi_powerSeries (k := k)
  IsIntegralClosure.of_isIntegrallyClosedIn

/-! ### The normalization -/

/-- **The normalization of the two-branch multicross ring is `k[[t]] × k[[t]]`.** The integral
closure of `A = {(f, g) ∈ k[[t]] × k[[t]] : f(0) = g(0)}` in its total ring of fractions is the
whole of `k[[t]] × k[[t]]`, as an `A`-algebra.

Together with `Stacks.Curves.quotientAlgEquivMulticross` this computes the normalization of
`k[[x, y]]/(xy)`, which is Case I of the proof of Stacks Tag 0C4A. -/
def integralClosureMulticrossAlgEquiv (k : Type u) [Field k] :
    ↥(integralClosure ↥(multicross k 2) (FractionRing ↥(multicross k 2))) ≃ₐ[↥(multicross k 2)]
      (Fin 2 → PowerSeries k) :=
  letI := isFractionRing_multicross (k := k)
  letI := isIntegralClosure_pi_powerSeries (k := k)
  let e : FractionRing ↥(multicross k 2) ≃ₐ[↥(multicross k 2)]
      (Fin 2 → FractionRing (PowerSeries k)) :=
    IsLocalization.algEquiv (nonZeroDivisors ↥(multicross k 2)) _ _
  e.mapIntegralClosure.trans
    (IsIntegralClosure.equiv ↥(multicross k 2)
      (↥(integralClosure ↥(multicross k 2) (Fin 2 → FractionRing (PowerSeries k))))
      (Fin 2 → FractionRing (PowerSeries k)) (Fin 2 → PowerSeries k))

/-! ### The split quadric over an arbitrary field -/

/-- **Over any field, a nondegenerate binary quadratic form with a nontrivial zero is a product
of two linearly independent linear forms.** This is
`Stacks.Curves.exists_linearFactorization_of_nondegenerate` with algebraic closedness of the
base field replaced by the hypothesis that the form splits there, which is what Case I of the
proof of Stacks Tag 0C4A assumes: the nontrivial zero is one of the two lines. -/
theorem exists_linearFactorization_of_isotropic (a b c : k) (h : 4 * a * c - b ^ 2 ≠ 0)
    (hsplit : ∃ u v : k, (u ≠ 0 ∨ v ≠ 0) ∧ a * u ^ 2 + b * u * v + c * v ^ 2 = 0) :
    ∃ α β γ δ : k, α * δ - β * γ ≠ 0 ∧ α * γ = a ∧ α * δ + β * γ = b ∧ β * δ = c := by
  rcases eq_or_ne a 0 with rfl | ha
  · have hb : b ≠ 0 := by
      intro hb
      simp [hb] at h
    exact ⟨0, 1, b, c, by simpa using hb, by ring, by ring, by ring⟩
  · obtain ⟨u, v, huv, hq⟩ := hsplit
    have hv : v ≠ 0 := by
      intro hv0
      have hu : u ≠ 0 := huv.resolve_right (by simp [hv0])
      rw [hv0] at hq
      have hau : a * u ^ 2 = 0 := by linear_combination hq
      exact ha ((mul_eq_zero.1 hau).resolve_right (pow_ne_zero 2 hu))
    set z : k := -u / v with hzdef
    have hzv : z * v = -u := by
      rw [hzdef]
      field_simp
    have hz : a * z ^ 2 - b * z + c = 0 := by
      have h2 : (a * z ^ 2 - b * z + c) * v ^ 2 = 0 := by
        linear_combination hq + (a * (z * v - u) - b * v) * hzv
      exact (mul_eq_zero.1 h2).resolve_right (pow_ne_zero 2 hv)
    exact ⟨a, b - a * z, 1, z,
      fun h0 ↦ h (by linear_combination 4 * a * hz - (a * z - (b - a * z)) * h0),
      by ring, by ring, by linear_combination -hz⟩

/-- **Over any field, the power-series hypersurface cut out by a nondegenerate binary quadratic
form that splits is `k[[x, y]]/(xy)`.** This is the split-but-not-necessarily-algebraically-
closed form of `Stacks.Curves.nonempty_quotient_nodalBinaryQuadratic_algEquiv`, and is the step
"after changing coordinates we may assume `q = xy`" of Case I of Stacks Tag 0C4A. -/
theorem nonempty_quotient_nodalBinaryQuadratic_algEquiv_of_isotropic (a b c : k)
    (h : 4 * a * c - b ^ 2 ≠ 0)
    (hsplit : ∃ u v : k, (u ≠ 0 ∨ v ≠ 0) ∧ a * u ^ 2 + b * u * v + c * v ^ 2 = 0) :
    Nonempty ((MvPowerSeries (Fin 2) k ⧸ Ideal.span {nodalBinaryQuadratic a b c}) ≃ₐ[k]
      (MvPowerSeries (Fin 2) k ⧸
        Ideal.span {(MvPowerSeries.X 0 : MvPowerSeries (Fin 2) k) * MvPowerSeries.X 1})) := by
  obtain ⟨α, β, γ, δ, hdet, ha, hb, hc⟩ := exists_linearFactorization_of_isotropic a b c h hsplit
  refine ⟨(Ideal.quotientEquivAlg
    (Ideal.span {(MvPowerSeries.X 0 : MvPowerSeries (Fin 2) k) * MvPowerSeries.X 1})
    (Ideal.span {nodalBinaryQuadratic a b c}) (linearSubstAlgEquiv α β γ δ hdet) ?_).symm⟩
  rw [Ideal.map_span, Set.image_singleton]
  congr 1
  rw [show ((linearSubstAlgEquiv α β γ δ hdet : _ →+* _)
      ((MvPowerSeries.X 0 : MvPowerSeries (Fin 2) k) * MvPowerSeries.X 1)) =
      linearSubstAlgEquiv α β γ δ hdet
        ((MvPowerSeries.X 0 : MvPowerSeries (Fin 2) k) * MvPowerSeries.X 1) from rfl,
    linearSubstAlgEquiv_apply, subst_linearSubst_X_mul_X, ha, hb, hc]

/-- **Over any field, the power-series hypersurface cut out by a nondegenerate binary quadratic
form that splits is the two-branch multicross ring.** This is
`Stacks.Curves.nonempty_algEquiv_multicross_of_nondegenerate` with algebraic closedness replaced
by splitting. -/
theorem nonempty_algEquiv_multicross_of_isotropic (a b c : k) (h : 4 * a * c - b ^ 2 ≠ 0)
    (hsplit : ∃ u v : k, (u ≠ 0 ∨ v ≠ 0) ∧ a * u ^ 2 + b * u * v + c * v ^ 2 = 0) :
    Nonempty ((MvPowerSeries (Fin 2) k ⧸ Ideal.span {nodalBinaryQuadratic a b c}) ≃ₐ[k]
      ↥(multicross k 2)) :=
  (nonempty_quotient_nodalBinaryQuadratic_algEquiv_of_isotropic a b c h hsplit).map fun e ↦
    e.trans quotientAlgEquivMulticross

/-! ### The delta invariant -/

/-- The image of the multicross ring in `k[[t]] × k[[t]]` is the multicross ring. -/
theorem mem_range_algebraMap_multicross_iff {x : Fin 2 → PowerSeries k} :
    x ∈ LinearMap.range (Algebra.linearMap ↥(multicross k 2) (Fin 2 → PowerSeries k)) ↔
      x ∈ multicross k 2 := by
  constructor
  · rintro ⟨y, rfl⟩
    exact y.2
  · exact fun hx ↦ ⟨⟨x, hx⟩, rfl⟩

/-- **The image of the two-branch multicross ring is a coatom in the lattice of submodules of
`k[[t]] × k[[t]]`.** A pair not lying in the multicross ring has, after subtracting an element of
the multicross ring, the shape `c · (1, 0)` with `c` a nonzero constant, hence invertible in the
multicross ring; so any submodule properly containing the multicross ring contains the
idempotent `(1, 0)`, and with it everything. -/
theorem isCoatom_range_algebraMap_multicross :
    IsCoatom (LinearMap.range (Algebra.linearMap ↥(multicross k 2)
      (Fin 2 → PowerSeries k))) := by
  constructor
  · intro htop
    have he : firstBranchIdem k ∈ multicross k 2 :=
      mem_range_algebraMap_multicross_iff.1 (htop ▸ Submodule.mem_top)
    have h01 : PowerSeries.constantCoeff (firstBranchIdem k 0) =
        PowerSeries.constantCoeff (firstBranchIdem k 1) := he 0 1
    simp [firstBranchIdem] at h01
  · intro P hP
    obtain ⟨x, hxP, hxN⟩ := SetLike.exists_of_lt hP
    rw [mem_range_algebraMap_multicross_iff, mem_multicross] at hxN
    have hc : PowerSeries.constantCoeff (x 0) - PowerSeries.constantCoeff (x 1) ≠ 0 := by
      intro h0
      refine hxN fun i j ↦ ?_
      have hx01 : PowerSeries.constantCoeff (x 0) = PowerSeries.constantCoeff (x 1) := by
        linear_combination h0
      fin_cases i <;> fin_cases j <;> simp_all
    obtain ⟨a, hsplit⟩ := exists_smul_one_add_smul_firstBranchIdem x
    set c : k := PowerSeries.constantCoeff (x 0) - PowerSeries.constantCoeff (x 1) with hcdef
    have hcA := mem_multicross_const (k := k) (PowerSeries.C c)
    have hcinvA := mem_multicross_const (k := k) (PowerSeries.C c⁻¹)
    have hone : (1 : Fin 2 → PowerSeries k) ∈ P :=
      hP.le (mem_range_algebraMap_multicross_iff.2 (multicross k 2).one_mem)
    have hce : (⟨_, hcA⟩ : ↥(multicross k 2)) • firstBranchIdem k ∈ P := by
      rw [eq_sub_of_add_eq' hsplit]
      exact P.sub_mem hxP (P.smul_mem a hone)
    have hidem : firstBranchIdem k ∈ P := by
      have hmul := P.smul_mem (⟨_, hcinvA⟩ : ↥(multicross k 2)) hce
      refine (show (⟨_, hcinvA⟩ : ↥(multicross k 2)) • ((⟨_, hcA⟩ : ↥(multicross k 2)) •
        firstBranchIdem k) = firstBranchIdem k from ?_) ▸ hmul
      refine funext fun i ↦ ?_
      rw [multicross_smul_apply, multicross_smul_apply, ← mul_assoc, ← map_mul,
        inv_mul_cancel₀ hc, map_one, one_mul]
    refine top_le_iff.1 ?_
    rw [← span_one_firstBranchIdem_eq_top (k := k), Submodule.span_le]
    rintro _ (rfl | rfl)
    exacts [hone, hidem]

/-- **The delta invariant of the two-branch multicross ring is `1`.** This is the verification
"by hand" in Case I of the proof of Stacks Tag 0C4A that `δ = 1` for a node: the normalization
is `k[[t]] × k[[t]]`, and the quotient of that by the multicross ring is a simple module,
spanned by the class of the idempotent `(1, 0)`. -/
@[sga "7 X 2.24" "partial"]
theorem deltaInvariant_multicross : Ring.deltaInvariant ↥(multicross k 2) = 1 := by
  have hmap : (LinearMap.range (Algebra.linearMap ↥(multicross k 2)
        ↥(integralClosure ↥(multicross k 2) (FractionRing ↥(multicross k 2))))).map
      ((integralClosureMulticrossAlgEquiv k).toLinearEquiv :
        ↥(integralClosure ↥(multicross k 2) (FractionRing ↥(multicross k 2))) →ₗ[_]
          (Fin 2 → PowerSeries k)) =
      LinearMap.range (Algebra.linearMap ↥(multicross k 2) (Fin 2 → PowerSeries k)) := by
    rw [← LinearMap.range_comp]
    refine congrArg LinearMap.range (LinearMap.ext fun a ↦ ?_)
    exact (integralClosureMulticrossAlgEquiv k).commutes a
  have hlen := (Submodule.Quotient.equiv _ _
    (integralClosureMulticrossAlgEquiv k).toLinearEquiv hmap).length_eq
  exact hlen.trans (Module.length_eq_one_iff.2
    (isSimpleModule_iff_isCoatom.2 isCoatom_range_algebraMap_multicross))

/-! ### The two maximal ideals of the normalization -/

/-- The maximal ideal of `k[[t]] × k[[t]]` supported on the `i`-th branch. -/
def branchMaximalIdeal (k : Type u) [Field k] (i : Fin 2) : Ideal (Fin 2 → PowerSeries k) :=
  (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal (PowerSeries k)).comap
    (Pi.evalRingHom (fun _ : Fin 2 ↦ PowerSeries k) i)

/-- A pair lies in the `i`-th branch maximal ideal if and only if its `i`-th component is not
a unit. -/
theorem mem_branchMaximalIdeal {i : Fin 2} {f : Fin 2 → PowerSeries k} :
    f ∈ branchMaximalIdeal k i ↔ ¬ IsUnit (f i) :=
  IsLocalRing.mem_maximalIdeal _

/-- The `i`-th branch maximal ideal of `k[[t]] × k[[t]]` is a maximal ideal. -/
instance isMaximal_branchMaximalIdeal (i : Fin 2) : (branchMaximalIdeal k i).IsMaximal :=
  Ideal.comap_isMaximal_of_surjective _ fun r ↦ ⟨fun _ ↦ r, rfl⟩

/-- A proper ideal containing an element that is `1` off the `i`-th branch and `0` on it lies in
the `i`-th branch maximal ideal. -/
private theorem le_branchMaximalIdeal_of_mem (M : Ideal (Fin 2 → PowerSeries k)) [hM : M.IsMaximal]
    (i : Fin 2) {e : Fin 2 → PowerSeries k} (he : e ∈ M) (h1 : ∀ l, l ≠ i → e l = 1)
    (h0 : e i = 0) : M ≤ branchMaximalIdeal k i := fun f hf ↦ by
  refine mem_branchMaximalIdeal.2 fun hunit ↦ hM.ne_top
    (Ideal.eq_top_of_isUnit_mem M (M.add_mem he (M.mul_mem_left (1 - e) hf)) ?_)
  refine Pi.isUnit_iff.2 fun l ↦ ?_
  by_cases hl : l = i
  · subst hl
    simpa [h0] using hunit
  · simp [h1 l hl]

/-- **A maximal ideal of `k[[t]] × k[[t]]` is one of the two branch maximal ideals.** The two
branch idempotents multiply to zero, so a prime ideal contains one of them; and an ideal
containing one of them together with an element invertible on the other branch contains a
unit. -/
theorem eq_branchMaximalIdeal (M : Ideal (Fin 2 → PowerSeries k)) [hM : M.IsMaximal] :
    M = branchMaximalIdeal k 0 ∨ M = branchMaximalIdeal k 1 := by
  have hmul : firstBranchIdem k * (1 - firstBranchIdem k) = 0 := by
    refine funext fun i ↦ ?_
    fin_cases i <;> simp [firstBranchIdem]
  rcases hM.isPrime.mem_or_mem (hmul ▸ M.zero_mem) with hmem | hmem
  · refine Or.inr (hM.eq_of_le (isMaximal_branchMaximalIdeal (k := k) 1).ne_top
      (le_branchMaximalIdeal_of_mem M 1 hmem (fun l hl ↦ ?_) (by simp [firstBranchIdem])))
    fin_cases l <;> simp_all [firstBranchIdem]
  · refine Or.inl (hM.eq_of_le (isMaximal_branchMaximalIdeal (k := k) 0).ne_top
      (le_branchMaximalIdeal_of_mem M 0 hmem (fun l hl ↦ ?_) (by simp [firstBranchIdem])))
    fin_cases l <;> simp_all [firstBranchIdem]

/-- The two branch maximal ideals of `k[[t]] × k[[t]]` are distinct. -/
theorem branchMaximalIdeal_ne :
    branchMaximalIdeal k 0 ≠ branchMaximalIdeal k 1 := by
  intro h
  have h1 : firstBranchIdem k ∈ branchMaximalIdeal k 1 :=
    mem_branchMaximalIdeal.2 (by simp [firstBranchIdem])
  have h0 : firstBranchIdem k ∈ branchMaximalIdeal k 0 := h ▸ h1
  exact (mem_branchMaximalIdeal.1 h0) (by simp [firstBranchIdem])

/-- **`k[[t]] × k[[t]]` has exactly two maximal ideals.** With
`Stacks.Curves.integralClosureMulticrossAlgEquiv` this is the count of maximal ideals of the
normalization of a node in Case I of Stacks Tag 0C4A. -/
theorem encard_maximalSpectrum_pi_powerSeries :
    Set.encard (Set.univ : Set (MaximalSpectrum (Fin 2 → PowerSeries k))) = 2 := by
  have huniv : (Set.univ : Set (MaximalSpectrum (Fin 2 → PowerSeries k))) =
      {⟨branchMaximalIdeal k 0, isMaximal_branchMaximalIdeal 0⟩,
        ⟨branchMaximalIdeal k 1, isMaximal_branchMaximalIdeal 1⟩} := by
    refine Set.eq_of_subset_of_subset (fun m _ ↦ ?_) (fun _ _ ↦ Set.mem_univ _)
    rcases eq_branchMaximalIdeal m.asIdeal (hM := m.isMaximal) with h | h
    · exact Or.inl (MaximalSpectrum.ext h)
    · exact Or.inr (Set.mem_singleton_iff.2 (MaximalSpectrum.ext h))
  rw [huniv]
  exact Set.encard_pair fun h ↦ branchMaximalIdeal_ne (k := k) (congrArg MaximalSpectrum.asIdeal h)

end Stacks.Curves

